{"ts": 1789778593.688946, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign-flips behave exactly as the independence model requires (flipping signs on near-orthogonal features drops the RSS toward cancellation), confirming there's no hidden interaction structure. The features are essentially mutually orthogonal, so **18/17/27 with signs (\u2212,+,+) is genuinely the best available specification** \u2014 it simply stacks the three largest single effects.\n\nNow the honest OOS forecast. My in-sample 1.017 decomposes as \u221a3 \u00d7 (average single \u2248 0.59). The question is how much of that 0.59 survives.\n\nApplying shrinkage to the singles: with 40 candidates, SE \u2248 0.224, and an observed spread of single Sharpes only modestly wider than 0.224, the cross-sectional variance of true effects is small. Estimating var(observed) \u2248 0.26\u00b2 across the 40 singles, and subtracting the noise variance 0.224\u00b2, leaves true-effect SD \u2248 0.13. The shrinkage factor is then 0.13\u00b2/0.26\u00b2 \u2248 0.25. So a selected single of ~0.59 shrinks toward roughly 0.15, and even generously allowing that my top picks are partly real, I'd put the true per-feature Sharpe near 0.15\u20130.20.\n\nCombining three such features at \u221a3: expected OOS \u2248 \u221a3 \u00d7 0.17 \u2248 0.30.\n\nI'll set my mean slightly above that at 0.35 to hedge against over-shrinking (the top singles did clear the noise envelope somewhat), with a wide SD reflecting genuine uncertainty about the true effect sizes."}
