{"ts": 1789778642.537718, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features 18 (short), 17 (long), 27 (long) \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.017**, predicted OOS **0.35 \u00b1 0.30**.\n\n### How I got there\n\nI evaluated all 40 features individually, then explored pairs, triples, sign-flips, and \u2014 importantly \u2014 triples built from features I had *not* selected on.\n\n### The central finding\n\nThe features are essentially **mutually orthogonal**. Every combination's Sharpe matched the root-sum-square of its components to within a couple hundredths:\n\n- 18/17 \u2192 predicted \u221a(0.626\u00b2+0.605\u00b2) = 0.87, observed **0.864**\n- 18/17/27 \u2192 predicted 1.02, observed **1.017**\n\nThis mattered more than it might appear. The jump from ~0.6 singles to ~1.0 triples looks like discovery, but it's pure diversification arithmetic \u2014 mechanical, and available from *any* three features. Weak-feature triples rose by the same \u221a3 factor. So the combination step added **no information**, and pair/triple Sharpes gave me no independent evidence about which features are real. All the selection bias sits in the singles and gets amplified identically.\n\n### Why I predict far below the in-sample 1.017\n\nWith 40 candidates and n=5000 (SE \u2248 0.22 annualized), pure noise alone would produce a top single near 0.49. My best was 0.626 \u2014 above that, but not dramatically. Decomposing the cross-sectional spread of single Sharpes (SD \u2248 0.26) against noise (0.22) leaves a true-effect SD of only ~0.13, implying I should keep roughly a quarter of each selected single's magnitude.\n\nThat puts true per-feature Sharpe near 0.15\u20130.20, and \u221a3 \u00d7 0.17 \u2248 0.30. I nudged to 0.35 since the top singles did clear the noise envelope somewhat.\n\n**The in-sample 1.017 is roughly two-thirds selection bias.** The specification is still the right pick \u2014 orthogonality means stacking the three largest effects is genuinely optimal \u2014 but the honest expectation is a modest Sharpe near 0.35, and the wide SD reflects real uncertainty about whether these effects exist at all."}
